WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.179
This briefing is Book 09 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.179 --> 00:00:06.762
Network and Transport.

4
00:00:06.762 --> 00:00:11.885
It's about moving branch traffic off the
long hairpin through a distant data center,

5
00:00:11.885 --> 00:00:15.226
without turning a latency
gain into a compliance loss.

6
00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:19.100
Faster and governed are different
things, and the branch needs both.

7
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.914
We'll go in four steps: why
physics decides the architecture,

8
00:00:22.914 --> 00:00:26.650
what has to change, who owns the
network, and how to get there.

9
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.462
Here's the short version up front.

10
00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:30.799
No product shortens a fiber path.

11
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:34.287
So buy transport on physics,
and govern it as compliance.

12
00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:36.230
Let's start with the physics.

13
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.068
Physics decides the architecture
because part of every delay on a

14
00:00:41.068 --> 00:00:45.305
wide-area path is simply the time
light takes to travel through glass.

15
00:00:45.305 --> 00:00:50.405
Roughly, it's one millisecond for every
two hundred kilometers of fiber, one way.

16
00:00:50.405 --> 00:00:53.593
New York to London has a
round-trip floor of fifty-six

17
00:00:53.593 --> 00:00:56.855
milliseconds before any
equipment touches a packet.

18
00:00:56.855 --> 00:01:01.342
Optimization works on the gap above
that floor, never on the floor itself.

19
00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:03.131
There's a common misreading too.

20
00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:07.055
The often-quoted 150-millisecond
voice budget is mouth to ear,

21
00:01:07.055 --> 00:01:09.980
and the phones themselves
use a big share of it.

22
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.249
So a West Coast office hairpinning
through northern Virginia

23
00:01:13.249 --> 00:01:16.392
can spend most of the network's
allowance on the detour alone.

24
00:01:16.392 --> 00:01:20.892
And a synchronous commit pays a round
trip every time, whatever the bandwidth.

25
00:01:20.892 --> 00:01:25.792
The answer is needing less global
synchronization, not faster synchronization.

26
00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:28.130
Which leads to a key distinction.

27
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.367
Programs keep merging two decisions
that should stay separate.

28
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:36.142
Choosing a pipe, whether
fiber, MPLS, satellite,

29
00:01:36.142 --> 00:01:39.718
5G or direct internet,
is a physics decision.

30
00:01:39.718 --> 00:01:42.967
Choosing what governs the pipe
is a compliance decision.

31
00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:47.180
Direct internet access solves
exactly one problem, the hairpin.

32
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.984
It satisfies none of
the protection controls,

33
00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:53.305
like boundary protection,
inspection and monitoring.

34
00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:56.510
So moving a branch from a
governed hairpin to bare local

35
00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.855
breakout is a compliance
regression with better latency.

36
00:01:59.855 --> 00:02:02.505
And the governor is itself attack surface.

37
00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:06.573
Federal directives already target
end-of-support edge devices,

38
00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:10.410
and an emergency directive in
February 2026 ordered action

39
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:14.017
on SD-WAN systems after
attackers were found inside them.

40
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:18.143
So protect the controller and every
edge before they carry traffic.

41
00:02:18.143 --> 00:02:21.070
Next, what policy actually requires.

42
00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.607
Read from the documents, TIC 3.0 describes
goals; it doesn't prescribe methods.

43
00:02:27.607 --> 00:02:30.633
It has four use cases,
and none of them is SASE.

44
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:35.962
Two statements matter most for a branch:
the same policy enforcement on every path,

45
00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:39.958
and keeping CISA's visibility
as branches connect directly.

46
00:02:39.958 --> 00:02:41.595
Logging is the other half.

47
00:02:41.595 --> 00:02:46.488
The current federal logging memo
sets retention and content rules,

48
00:02:46.488 --> 00:02:50.595
and the first agency logging
plan is due November 18, 2026.

49
00:02:50.595 --> 00:02:51.670
Here's the trap.

50
00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.242
When branches leave the managed
trusted internet service,

51
00:02:55.242 --> 00:02:57.720
the telemetry CISA was receiving stops.

52
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.989
The replacement is CISA's
log aggregation warehouse,

53
00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:05.420
and onboarding needs the agency, CISA
and the provider working together.

54
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.757
So keep the old service
until CISA confirms parity.

55
00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:11.382
Technology-neutral isn't requirement-free.

56
00:03:11.382 --> 00:03:14.820
Four binding obligations still
land on branch equipment.

57
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.750
Now, where data and trust should live.

58
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.337
The book separates two
meanings of centralize.

59
00:03:21.337 --> 00:03:25.489
Logical centralization means one
authoritative source of truth,

60
00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:29.263
with every other copy derived,
and that's almost always right.

61
00:03:29.263 --> 00:03:33.137
Physical centralization means
one server in one place,

62
00:03:33.137 --> 00:03:38.612
and it runs into three walls: the speed of
light, blast radius and data residency.

63
00:03:38.612 --> 00:03:42.388
Tokens remove the strongest
old reason to fragment.

64
00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:46.102
When every request carries
a verifiable identity,

65
00:03:46.102 --> 00:03:49.463
one store can safely
serve many trust domains.

66
00:03:49.463 --> 00:03:53.945
Going out, a service can sign in to
its database with a short-lived token,

67
00:03:53.945 --> 00:03:57.037
so the long-lived database
password disappears.

68
00:03:57.037 --> 00:03:59.887
The price is that isolation
now lives in code.

69
00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:03.631
Then pick the weakest consistency
each flow can tolerate,

70
00:04:03.631 --> 00:04:07.375
because strong consistency
across regions is expensive.

71
00:04:07.375 --> 00:04:10.300
Next, the tools that govern the path.

72
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.550
SD-WAN is the governor's first layer.

73
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:18.652
It splits traffic into a lean class, like
voice and sign-in, and a bulk class, like

74
00:04:18.652 --> 00:04:23.913
backups and patches, so big transfers
don't queue ahead of time-critical packets.

75
00:04:23.913 --> 00:04:28.066
It also recovers wasted routing:
Microsoft measured paths well

76
00:04:28.066 --> 00:04:31.750
above the fiber floor, and SD-WAN
can close much of that gap.

77
00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.663
It reaches the floor;
it never goes below it.

78
00:04:34.663 --> 00:04:39.926
SASE sits on top, with web gateway,
firewall and zero trust access.

79
00:04:39.926 --> 00:04:44.014
CISA mentions SD-WAN as part
of SASE but mandates neither,

80
00:04:44.014 --> 00:04:48.687
and SD-WAN alone doesn't close the
boundary and inspection controls.

81
00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:49.851
Two cautions.

82
00:04:49.851 --> 00:04:52.030
The enforcement point has a location,

83
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.038
so a distant point of presence is
just a hairpin with a different name.

84
00:04:56.038 --> 00:04:59.090
And FedRAMP status differs
by offering, not by vendor,

85
00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.350
so check the Marketplace when
you write the requirement.

86
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:06.020
Re-routing also breaks
things, for known reasons.

87
00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.346
Re-routing a branch breaks things
for a short list of reasons,

88
00:05:10.346 --> 00:05:11.920
so design around that list.

89
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.460
Proxies drop idle connections, TLS
inspection breaks pinned certificates,

90
00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:20.783
and changing a client's apparent
source address resets its connections.

91
00:05:20.783 --> 00:05:23.244
Address continuity is what matters.

92
00:05:23.244 --> 00:05:24.945
Two myths get cleared up here.

93
00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:29.257
Kerberos works through address
translation under Windows defaults.

94
00:05:29.257 --> 00:05:31.987
And there's no
five-millisecond requirement;

95
00:05:31.987 --> 00:05:34.495
the five is minutes of allowed clock skew.

96
00:05:34.495 --> 00:05:37.908
What really needs a reachable
domain controller is short,

97
00:05:37.908 --> 00:05:39.970
like first sign-in and Group Policy.

98
00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:44.192
Leave that path out and everything
works except signing in,

99
00:05:44.192 --> 00:05:47.207
while Kerberos quietly falls back to NTLM.

100
00:05:47.207 --> 00:05:50.482
And for chatty applications,
round trips dominate.

101
00:05:50.482 --> 00:05:54.581
On the book's illustrative numbers,
a better path saves maybe a fifth,

102
00:05:54.581 --> 00:05:59.045
but cutting thirty round trips to three
saves about an order of magnitude.

103
00:05:59.045 --> 00:06:01.910
Next, proving the device on the wire.

104
00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.380
Every token in this
book assumes the device

105
00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.798
on the wire is the device it claims to be.

106
00:06:07.798 --> 00:06:12.773
802.1X turns that assumption
into proof at the switch port.

107
00:06:12.773 --> 00:06:16.516
Use certificate-based authentication
for every managed device;

108
00:06:16.516 --> 00:06:20.885
the password methods are already being
broken by Windows security changes.

109
00:06:20.885 --> 00:06:23.619
The RADIUS server is
usually the last piece of

110
00:06:23.619 --> 00:06:26.497
the network still standing
on Active Directory.

111
00:06:26.497 --> 00:06:29.905
Windows NPS checks against
AD computer objects,

112
00:06:29.905 --> 00:06:32.547
which Entra-joined devices don't have.

113
00:06:32.547 --> 00:06:36.948
There's no Entra RADIUS product,
and NPS has no device health engine.

114
00:06:36.948 --> 00:06:43.008
So posture comes from Intune compliance,
read by a network access control platform,

115
00:06:43.008 --> 00:06:45.907
and each device lands in the right place:

116
00:06:45.907 --> 00:06:49.509
production, remediation,
quarantine, guest or IoT.

117
00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:54.210
And after the Blast-RADIUS attack,
require message authentication everywhere.

118
00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.480
Now we can put the pieces together.

119
00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:59.801
The pieces come together under one rule:

120
00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:03.618
match each flow's consistency
needs to its transport class.

121
00:07:03.618 --> 00:07:07.944
Only authoritative writes and
in-region quorum ride the lean path,

122
00:07:07.944 --> 00:07:10.855
and neither crosses a
region synchronously.

123
00:07:10.855 --> 00:07:13.592
At the branch, four
patterns cover practice.

124
00:07:13.592 --> 00:07:17.493
The hairpin is assessed, but
every flow pays the detour.

125
00:07:17.493 --> 00:07:21.554
Bare local breakout isn't an
option in the TIC use case at all.

126
00:07:21.554 --> 00:07:23.251
Governed local breakout,

127
00:07:23.251 --> 00:07:27.530
an SD-WAN edge plus a nearby SASE
point, is the reference pattern.

128
00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:31.355
And agent-delivered SASE
suits small and roaming sites.

129
00:07:31.355 --> 00:07:35.396
Then five decisions carry most of
the risk: one writer per region,

130
00:07:35.396 --> 00:07:39.057
a written path policy, the
boundary change before cutover,

131
00:07:39.057 --> 00:07:44.242
the governor protected before it carries
traffic, and the path made into evidence.

132
00:07:44.242 --> 00:07:47.155
Each fails quietly, so
each comes with a check.

133
00:07:47.155 --> 00:07:49.100
Who makes those decisions?

134
00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:53.475
The most expensive mistakes in
this book are ownership gaps.

135
00:07:53.475 --> 00:07:56.350
A circuit bought before
anyone owned the governor.

136
00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.700
A cutover before anyone
filed the boundary change.

137
00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.662
A telemetry contract cancelled before
its replacement was confirmed.

138
00:08:03.662 --> 00:08:06.288
So the rule is one decider per decision.

139
00:08:06.288 --> 00:08:11.551
The sponsor and CIO choose the governor
platforms and the order of branch waves.

140
00:08:11.551 --> 00:08:16.587
The authorizing official decides the boundary
change and every standing exception.

141
00:08:16.587 --> 00:08:21.526
The CISO sets the control requirements
and confirms CISA telemetry parity.

142
00:08:21.526 --> 00:08:24.023
The system owner decides, site by site,

143
00:08:24.023 --> 00:08:28.213
whether the governor is live and
logging before traffic moves.

144
00:08:28.213 --> 00:08:31.630
Security architecture
decides TIC conformance

145
00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.463
and writes the target into every refresh.

146
00:08:34.463 --> 00:08:38.812
And only a contracting officer
can enter into or end a contract,

147
00:08:38.812 --> 00:08:41.238
so no engineer can cancel a circuit.

148
00:08:41.238 --> 00:08:44.820
Next, how to get there when
the target is years away.

149
00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:49.958
When the target is years away, the
book makes the case by elimination.

150
00:08:49.958 --> 00:08:52.596
The legacy telecom contracts have ended.

151
00:08:52.596 --> 00:08:54.391
The hairpin is still allowed,

152
00:08:54.391 --> 00:08:57.983
but CISA itself says it raises
the cost of using the cloud.

153
00:08:57.983 --> 00:09:01.123
A new access point is a
big capital commitment,

154
00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:03.933
and VPN backhaul is just another hairpin.

155
00:09:03.933 --> 00:09:05.717
What's left is local breakout,

156
00:09:05.717 --> 00:09:09.358
a cloud security stack and a
policy layer at the branch edge.

157
00:09:09.358 --> 00:09:13.307
Meanwhile several federal deadlines
compete for the same staff.

158
00:09:13.307 --> 00:09:14.471
Here's the fork.

159
00:09:14.471 --> 00:09:18.753
A directive requires end-of-support
edge devices off agency

160
00:09:18.753 --> 00:09:23.208
networks by about August 2027, so
a refresh is happening anyway.

161
00:09:23.208 --> 00:09:26.983
Tell whoever scopes it, in
writing, what the target needs.

162
00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:29.886
A like-for-like refresh
spends the same money and

163
00:09:29.886 --> 00:09:33.007
locks in the old design for
the life of the equipment.

164
00:09:33.007 --> 00:09:35.720
A worked example shows what's at stake.

165
00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:41.447
This worked case is a large field-office
estate, about fifteen hundred offices,

166
00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:44.433
asked to fund a
fifty-million-dollar refresh of

167
00:09:44.433 --> 00:09:47.495
a software distribution
server in every office.

168
00:09:47.495 --> 00:09:50.990
Microsoft's published limit
means the whole estate needs

169
00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.120
only about two dozen of those
servers, at a dozen hubs.

170
00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.595
The rest exist only to make
up for slow branch circuits.

171
00:09:57.595 --> 00:10:01.794
So fix the circuit instead:
right-sized internet access,

172
00:10:01.794 --> 00:10:04.533
a 5G backup path and an SD-WAN overlay.

173
00:10:04.533 --> 00:10:08.426
On the book's planning assumptions,
the network program needs about

174
00:10:08.426 --> 00:10:12.320
ten million dollars of first-year
capital and pays back in year four.

175
00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.645
But the case turns on one number,
the current MPLS cost per office.

176
00:10:16.645 --> 00:10:20.645
Below the breakeven, argue
capability, not savings.

177
00:10:20.645 --> 00:10:25.945
And no office's MPLS is retired until
its replacement telemetry is confirmed.

178
00:10:25.945 --> 00:10:29.060
Next, one exception
that needs a signature.

179
00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:33.163
Microsoft says to send
its most latency-sensitive

180
00:10:33.163 --> 00:10:36.835
traffic straight to Microsoft,
without TLS inspection.

181
00:10:36.835 --> 00:10:39.047
The boundary says inspect everything.

182
00:10:39.047 --> 00:10:43.322
Both positions hold up, so the gap
is a decision someone has to sign.

183
00:10:43.322 --> 00:10:48.934
The Optimize traffic is small: Exchange,
SharePoint and OneDrive, and Teams media.

184
00:10:48.934 --> 00:10:52.285
Exempting only that is
narrower than Microsoft asks,

185
00:10:52.285 --> 00:10:54.873
and it keeps sign-in
on the inspected path.

186
00:10:54.873 --> 00:10:59.572
The book names exactly which controls
the bypass weakens, and the vehicle

187
00:10:59.572 --> 00:11:04.348
is a documented boundary exception,
with mission need, duration and review.

188
00:11:04.348 --> 00:11:08.110
An assessor's main worry will be
data leaving to another tenant.

189
00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:11.638
Only device-side tenant
restrictions reach that data path,

190
00:11:11.638 --> 00:11:13.798
so they're a precondition to cutover.

191
00:11:13.798 --> 00:11:17.573
Three signed documents carry the
decision, and the notes name them.

192
00:11:17.573 --> 00:11:20.290
All of this depends on
getting the order right.

193
00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:24.928
Order decides whether modernization
is a gain or a regression.

194
00:11:24.928 --> 00:11:29.246
Step zero is bringing the governor
under vulnerability management,

195
00:11:29.246 --> 00:11:31.215
privileged access and logging.

196
00:11:31.215 --> 00:11:33.564
Then buy the pipe on measured physics,

197
00:11:33.564 --> 00:11:36.596
deploy the governor before
production traffic,

198
00:11:36.596 --> 00:11:41.826
decide the boundary change before the
first cutover, handle Microsoft 365 through

199
00:11:41.826 --> 00:11:46.752
its own exception, and confirm CISA
telemetry before any legacy circuit ends.

200
00:11:46.752 --> 00:11:50.665
No memo mandates that order;
it's an engineering dependency.

201
00:11:50.665 --> 00:11:55.838
Get it wrong and you get phase inversion:
cloud migration runs ahead of transport,

202
00:11:55.838 --> 00:11:59.235
slowness gets blamed on the
cloud, cloud spend goes up,

203
00:11:59.235 --> 00:12:02.015
and the real cause never
shows up in the data.

204
00:12:02.015 --> 00:12:05.915
The book is careful to call that
an inference, not a proven fact.

205
00:12:05.915 --> 00:12:07.677
So measure in your own estate.

206
00:12:07.677 --> 00:12:12.772
Instrument two comparable branches for thirty
days before and after a change at one,

207
00:12:12.772 --> 00:12:15.828
and you get evidence a
budget examiner will accept.

208
00:12:15.828 --> 00:12:17.810
So, the decisions.

209
00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:20.160
Here are the four decisions.

210
00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.459
First, adopt the sequence as policy: the
governor secured and live before traffic,

211
00:12:25.459 --> 00:12:28.385
the boundary change
decided before cutover,

212
00:12:28.385 --> 00:12:32.972
and CISA telemetry parity confirmed
before any legacy circuit ends.

213
00:12:32.972 --> 00:12:37.497
Second, put the target in writing
for every edge-device replacement,

214
00:12:37.497 --> 00:12:41.698
before the August 2027 deadline
spends the money like for like.

215
00:12:41.698 --> 00:12:45.682
Third, decide the Microsoft
365 Optimize exception,

216
00:12:45.682 --> 00:12:50.036
with device-side tenant
restrictions as a precondition.

217
00:12:50.036 --> 00:12:52.559
And fourth, name one decider per decision,

218
00:12:52.559 --> 00:12:56.197
from the authorizing official
to the contracting officer.

219
00:12:56.197 --> 00:13:00.807
The next step is to write the
application-to-path policy the boundary controls

220
00:13:00.807 --> 00:13:04.636
already ask for, and to instrument
a sample of branches before the

221
00:13:04.636 --> 00:13:08.748
next migration wave, because that
baseline can't be rebuilt afterward.

222
00:13:08.748 --> 00:13:11.785
Buy transport on physics
and govern it as compliance,

223
00:13:11.785 --> 00:13:14.685
and the branch gets faster
without getting weaker.

224
00:13:14.685 --> 00:13:18.710
Book 09 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

225
00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:19.540
Thank you.
